        "title": "Experimentation Program Basics",
        "content": "A great experimentation program isn't about running more tests — it's about running the right tests with rigor, velocity, and organizational buy-in. Here's what \"good\" looks like.",
        "bullets": [
          "PRIORITIZATION: Use ICE framework (Impact × Confidence × Ease) to rank test ideas — not HiPPO decisions",
          "RIGOR: Statistical significance at 95% confidence, adequate sample size, proper test duration (2+ weeks)",
          "VELOCITY: Goal of 8-10 tests/month across teams — requires shared infrastructure and governance",
          "CULTURE: Executive sponsorship, shared test calendar, \"learn from losses\" mindset, quarterly reviews",
          "GOVERNANCE: Clear ownership, test documentation, result archiving, and cross-team visibility",
          "Monetate enables this: Shared test calendar, auto-significance detection, test archive with insights"
        ],
        "imageUrl": "/uploads/slides/slide-maestro-experimentation-1-1771056135908.png",
        "imagePrompt": "Create a professional presentation slide with dark navy blue (#1A2B8C) background. Title in large white bold text: \"EXPERIMENTATION PROGRAM BASICS\". Show a \"maturity model\" diagram as 4 ascending levels. Level 1 (bottom): \"AD HOC — Random tests, no process\" in gray. Level 2: \"STRUCTURED — ICE prioritization, shared calendar\" in light blue. Level 3: \"SCALED — 8-10 tests/month, cross-team\" in white. Level 4 (top): \"CULTURE — Exec sponsorship, learn from losses\" in magenta (#E84C8A) with a star. Arrow on side shows progression. Right side shows key metrics: \"95% Confidence\", \"2+ Week Duration\", \"8-10 Tests/Month\". Bottom: \"WHERE IS YOUR PROSPECT? THAT'S YOUR OPENING.\" Professional maturity model style. 16:9 aspect ratio.",
        "narrationScript": "Alright, team, let’s dive into the essentials of a successful experimentation program. It’s not just about the sheer number of tests we run; it’s about running the *right* tests with the right approach. \n\nFirst up is **prioritization**. We want to utilize the ICE framework—Impact, Confidence, and Ease—to rank our test ideas. This will help us avoid making decisions based on HiPPO—Highest Paid Person’s Opinion—and instead rely on data-driven insights. \n\nNext, let’s talk about **rigor**. To ensure our tests are valid, we need to achieve a statistical significance of 95% confidence, maintain an adequate sample size, and run tests for at least two weeks. This rigor will give us the credibility we need in our findings.\n\nThen there’s **velocity**. Our goal should be to run 8 to 10 tests each month across our teams. Achieving this requires a shared infrastructure and strong governance to keep us aligned and efficient.\n\nNow, let’s focus on **culture**. Having executive sponsorship is crucial, along with a shared test calendar and a mindset that embraces learning from losses. Regular quarterly reviews will help us continuously improve our processes.\n\nFinally, we need solid **governance**. Clear ownership of tests, thorough documentation, and archiving results with cross-team visibility are essential for long-term success.\n\nAnd remember, tools like Monetate can facilitate this entire process with features like a shared test calendar and auto-significance detection.\n\nAs we move forward, let’s keep these principles in mind to strengthen our experimentation efforts and drive impactful results. Now, let's transition to how we can apply these concepts in our day-to-day selling strategies.",
