        "imagePrompt": "Create a professional presentation slide with dark navy blue (#1A2B8C) background. Title in large white bold text: \"ROLEPLAY: FEATURE EXPERIMENTATION\". Show a risk-reduction visual: a thermometer-style gauge going from \"HIGH RISK\" (red) at top to \"ZERO RISK\" (green) at bottom. Current state arrow at \"HIGH RISK\" labeled \"Big-Bang Releases, 4-Hour Rollback\". Target state arrow at \"ZERO RISK\" labeled \"Feature Flags, 30-Second Rollback\". Scenario: \"Head of Product, big-bang releases, 4-hour rollback pain.\" Bottom: \"GOAL: First feature flag test plan + rollback criteria.\" Professional product management slide. 16:9 aspect ratio.",
        "narrationScript": "Alright team, let’s dive into our roleplay scenario focused on feature experimentation. Imagine you’re in a meeting with a Head of Product who’s grappling with the risks of their current approach. They’re stuck in a cycle of big-bang releases, which not only take a lot of time but also come with those nerve-wracking 4-hour rollback cycles whenever something goes awry.\n\nYour mission here is to show them how feature flags and gradual rollouts can revolutionize their deployment strategy. This is all about positioning feature experimentation as the modern release strategy that keeps their team agile and their users happy. \n\nAs you engage with this persona, remember your goal: you need to define the first feature flag test plan and establish clear rollback criteria. This is crucial for demonstrating the safety and control that come with this approach, so they can feel confident in moving away from their traditional methods.\n\nThe key skill here is your ability to communicate effectively with Product and Engineering personas about safe deployment practices. You want to instill confidence and illustrate how these modern strategies minimize risk while allowing for innovation. \n\nSo, practice this scenario as if you’re having a real conversation. Think about the questions they might ask and how you can best respond to alleviate their concerns. As we move forward, keep this mindset of innovation and risk management in your toolkit, because our next topic will build on this foundation of effective communication and strategy.",
