        "imagePrompt": "Create a professional presentation slide with dark navy blue (#1A2B8C) background. Title in large white bold text: \"ROLEPLAY: FIRST MEETING AGENDA CONTROL\". Show a meeting agenda visual with two paths. Path A (crossed out in red): \"Jump to Demo\" → \"Feature Tour\" → \"No Clear Outcome\" → \"Lost Deal\". Path B (highlighted in magenta #E84C8A): \"Discovery First\" → \"Understand KPIs\" → \"Tailored Demo\" → \"Clear Next Steps\". A speech bubble shows the buyer saying \"Just show me the platform!\" with a strategy box: \"How do you redirect?\" Bottom: \"GOAL: Buyer agrees to needs → KPIs → demo flow.\" Professional strategy slide. 16:9 aspect ratio.",
        "narrationScript": "Alright, team, let’s dive into a critical scenario that you’re likely to encounter in the field. Imagine this: you walk into a first meeting, and right off the bat, the buyer says, “Just show me the platform.” It’s tempting, right? But here’s the key—we need to take control of the agenda and prioritize discovery before diving into a demo.\n\nOur primary goal in this situation is to secure a discovery-first agenda. This means we’re looking to understand their needs and establish success criteria before we showcase anything. Think about it—if we don’t know what they’re truly looking for, how can we tailor our demo to hit the mark?\n\nNow, what does passing this criteria look like? It’s simple, yet effective. We need the buyer to agree to a structured agenda that flows from understanding their needs, to discussing their key performance indicators, and then, and only then, moving into the demo. \n\nThis is where your skill in controlling the meeting comes into play. You want to guide the conversation confidently without coming across as overbearing. It’s a delicate balance, but mastering it will set you apart. \n\nSo, as you practice this roleplay, focus on leading the discussion toward that structured agenda. Remember, it’s all about building rapport while steering the conversation in a direction that benefits both you and the buyer. \n\nNow, let’s get ready to put these strategies into action as we move into our next section on effective discovery techniques!",
