        "imageUrl": "/uploads/slides/slide-solution-modules-3-1771057502990.png",
        "imagePrompt": "Create a professional presentation slide with dark navy blue (#1A2B8C) background. Title in large white bold text: \"EXPERIMENT TYPES\". Show 5 experiment type cards in a horizontal layout, each with a distinct magenta (#E84C8A) icon and use case. Card 1: \"A/B/n\" (split path icon) — \"Clear hypothesis, one variable\". Card 2: \"MULTIVARIATE\" (grid icon) — \"Complex redesigns\". Card 3: \"DYNAMIC\" (AI brain icon) — \"3x faster winners\". Card 4: \"FEATURE FLAGS\" (flag icon) — \"Safe launches\". Card 5: \"NETWORK-LAYER\" (network icon) — \"Regulated environments\". Below, a decision flow: \"Clear hypothesis? → A/B. Multiple variables? → Multivariate. Need speed? → Dynamic. New feature? → Flags. Regulated? → Network.\" Professional decision-tree style. 16:9 aspect ratio.",
        "narrationScript": "Alright, team, let’s dive into the different experiment types we can leverage to optimize our sales strategies. Remember, not all tests are created equal, and knowing which type to use will set us up for success.\n\nFirst up, we have A/B/n testing. This method allows us to split traffic between two to five variations. It’s particularly effective when we have a clear hypothesis focused on just one variable—think layout, call-to-action, or pricing. This is your go-to for straightforward scenarios.\n\nNext, let’s talk about multivariate testing. This approach lets us test multiple variables at once, making it ideal for complex page redesigns where many elements are in play. It’s a bit more intricate, but the insights can be incredibly valuable.\n\nNow, dynamic testing takes it up a notch. Here, AI steps in to allocate more traffic to the winning variants in real-time, giving us a significant advantage—up to three times faster time-to-winner. This is about agility and making decisions based on live data.\n\nWe also have feature flags, which allow us to toggle features for specific audiences. This is perfect for safe launches and gradual rollouts, ensuring we mitigate risks while getting feedback.\n\nLastly, there’s network-layer testing. This involves server-side testing at the infrastructure level, which is essential for regulated environments and performance-critical tests.\n\nAs a rule of thumb, start with A/B for clarity, graduate to dynamic for velocity, and use feature flags for your releases. Understanding these concepts will empower you to scope pilots effectively and set the right expectations with your clients.\n\nNow, let’s move on to how we can apply these insights to our customer interactions!",
